# Java Methods
-> block of code performing some action which runs only when it
is called.

function -> bark()
Methods -> obj.bark()

-> write once reuse many times , times save
-> Duplicate code reduces.

=> Types of method
   1. user-defined Methods
   2. Standard library Methods

=> How to declare Methods:
   public class Main {
      public int sum(int a , int b){
      // code to be executed
      }
   }

   Access          return    Method   ( parameter
   Modifiers        type     Name        list)


1. Method Signature
   Sum(int a , int b)
   ^
   |
  Method Name   + parameter list

2. Access specifier
    a. public   a.private  3.protected  4.Default

3.Method Body

4.return type
 1. The return keyword is used to send a value back to
    the place where the method was called.

Call a Method:
   welcome();

Example 1
 public class Main {
 static void welcome() {
 System.out.println("Welcome to Physics Wallah");
  }
 public static void main(String[] args) {
   welcome();
 }
 }

output:  Welcome to Physics Wallah

# static variable
static int b;
Single copy for whole class
here static keyword is use to call the method without making a object



# instance variable
int a ;
all object will make their own class

* Standard library methods - build in method in java library
   print()
   sqrt()
   floor()


# Packages
  it is collection of similar classes, subpackages , interfaces
  Example: java.util
           java.io
           java.net


public accessibility -> WithinClass , OutsideClass , OutsidePackageChild
                        , OutsidePackageNon-Child

protected  accessibility ->WithinClass , OutsideClass ,OutsidePackageChild
default accessibility -> WithinClass , OutsideClass
private accessibility -> WithinClass


# Constructor
  special method used to initialize objects
  constructor name = class name
  no return type


Static methods (no object needed to call) are sometimes informally
referred to as "functions" because they behave like independent utilities.
Instance methods (require an object to call) are strictly referred to
as methods.

